# Python Style (Fluent Python)

- Write idiomatic Python: clear, explicit, and concise.
- Prefer core data model features (`__iter__`, `__len__`, `__repr__`, `__enter__`/`__exit__`) over ad-hoc helper APIs when appropriate.
- Favor protocols and duck typing over deep inheritance trees.
- Use EAFP style (`try`/`except`) when it improves readability and avoids redundant checks.
- Prefer comprehensions and generator expressions to verbose loops when they stay readable.
- Use standard library tools (`dataclasses`, `collections`, `pathlib`, `itertools`, `functools`) before custom utilities.
- Keep functions small and composable; avoid side effects in utility functions unless required.
- Use type hints where they clarify public contracts; avoid noisy or redundant annotations.
- Represent domain objects with meaningful types instead of loose dictionaries when possible.
- Avoid Java-style patterns in Python code (excessive getters/setters, unnecessary classes, boilerplate-heavy code).
